  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Wednesday) \nIssued at 1034 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nThe upper level trough over the PacNW will continue to eject \nnortheast through the start of the extended period while a shortwave \nat the base of the larger scale trough tracks from the Four Corners \ninto portions of the central-southern Plains. Meanwhile, a H5 jet \nwill set up to our north, helping to maintain a favorable \nenvironment for continued moisture transport into the region as flow \naloft becomes more southwesterly. PWATs are expected to remain well \nabove the 90th percentile of climatological normals, generally \nranging from 1.5\" to 1.75\" across the FA. With the combination of \nthe approaching shortwave and embedded perturbations within the \nenhanced upper level flow, and anomalously high moisture, increased \nstorm chances are expected on Friday. NBM probabilities show at \nleast a 40% chance of receiving 1\" or More of rainfall across the \nsouthwestern Texas Panhandle, where precipitation chances are the \nhighest, while probabilities for measurable precipitation of at \nleast 0.01\" range from 50 to 80% area-wide through Friday night. If \nstorms are able to develop, they will likely be scattered, tracking \nnortheastward through the region. The severe weather threat remains \nlow, though a well defined column of moisture and skinny CAPE \nprofiles on forecast soundings suggest locally heavy rainfall could \ncause flooding concerns. Storm motions look to be faster from what \nwe saw earlier this week, which should help mitigate some of the \nflood threat, although if storms begin to train across areas that \nhave already received decent rainfall, we could see the threat \nlocally increase across portions of the FA. \n\nHeading into the weekend, the upper level pattern will begin to \ntransition as an upper ridge builds back across the region behind \nthe departing disturbance. This will allow PWATS to gradually \ndecrease, resulting in a drying trend through the weekend. At the \nsame time, an uptick in geopotential heights and thickness values \nwill prompt warmer temperatures as the ridge strengthens over the \nregion. Resulting in temperatures back into the upper 80s to upper \n90s Saturday through Monday. This warm and drier trend does look to \nbe short-lived with ensemble guidance becoming increasingly \nconsistent in advertising another upper pattern shift by early next \nweek through mid-week. Along with ensembles, deterministic guidance \nis depicting our next big system tracking into the Four Corners \nregion by early next week, pushing a cold front through the region \nas it tracks eastward. Deterministic models are not in agreement in \nregards to the evolution of this system at this time, with the GFS \ndepicting a weaker and slower progression of the system, while the \nECMWF depicts a stronger-faster system. While the exact magnitude of \nthe system remains uncertain, ensemble agreement has improved \nsignificantly over the last 24 hours, lending a bit more confidence \nin cooler temperatures and renewed precipitation chances as we head \ninto early next week.",
